'''Retta''' (also called ''Reta'') is a [[Papuan languages|Papuan language]] spoken on [[Pura Island|Pura]] and Ternate islands, located between [[Pantar]] and [[Alor Island|Alor]] in the [[Alor archipelago]] of [[Indonesia]]. It is spoken by a few thousand people, and is considered an [[endangered language]] by ''[[Ethnologue]]''.<ref>{{Ethnologue28|ret|Reta}}</ref>

It is not related to but not mutually intelligible with [[Blagar language|Blagar]], which is spoken on the north side of Pura Island, and is unrelated to the [[Austronesian language]] [[Alorese language|Alorese]], which is spoken on the north side of Ternate.<ref name="Nash2020">{{cite journal |last1=Nash |first1=Joshua |last2=Bakker |first2=Peter |last3=Bøegh |first3=Kristoffer Friis |last4=Daval-Markussen |first4=Aymeric |last5=Haberland |first5=Hartmut |last6=Kedwards |first6=Dale |last7=Ladhams |first7=John |last8=Levisen |first8=Carsten |last9=Markússon |first9=Jón Símon |last10=Robbe |first10=Joost |last11=Willemsen |first11=Jeroen |title=On languages on islands |journal=Acta Linguistica Hafniensia |date=2 January 2020 |volume=52 |issue=1 |pages=81–116 |doi=10.1080/03740463.2020.1736747 |url=https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/portalfiles/portal/198863365/On_languages_on_islands_AAM.pdf |access-date=16 January 2026}}</ref>{{rp|9,15}}<ref name=Sulistyono2021>{{cite journal |last1=Sulistyono |first1=Yunus |title=Interpretación de la historia oral del pueblo alorese en Indonesia Oriental |journal=Revista Universidad y Sociedad |date=August 2021 |volume=13 |issue=4 |pages=339–350 |url=http://scielo.sld.cu/scielo.php?pid=S2218-36202021000400339&script=sci_arttext |access-date=16 January 2026 |issn=2218-3620}}</ref>{{rp|348-349}} One of its most distinctive features is a morphophonological process where some words can be intensified or made vulgar by replacing an {{IPAslink|l}} sound with an {{IPAblink|r}} sound.<ref name=Willemsen2020/>{{rp|191}}

==Classification==
Retta is a member of the [[Timor–Alor–Pantar languages]], a group of approximately 30 languages at the western edge of the [[Papuan languages]] (the non-Austronesian languages of New Guinea and surrounding islands). It is part of the [[Alor–Pantar languages|Alor–Pantar]] branch within the TAP languages. Bayesian analysis suggests that Retta is most closely related to the [[Blagar language]], followed by the [[Pura language]]. These three languages, called the "Straits languages", form a group apart from the Pantar languages.<ref name="Willemsen2021"/>{{rp|146-147}}<ref name="KK2022"/>{{rp|275,298-299}}

==Geographic distribution==
Retta is spoken in the [[Lesser Sunda Islands]] of [[Indonesia]]. It is primarily spoken on two small islands, [[Pura Island|Pura]] and Ternate{{efn|not to be confused with its more famous namesake, [[Ternate]] in the [[Maluku Islands]]}}, plus two recent settlements on the coast of the larger island of [[Alor Island|Alor]], facing toward Pura and Ternate.<ref name="Willemsen2021"/>{{rp|147}}

The language's namesake village, Retta, is on the southern coast of Pura.<ref name="Willemsen2021"/>{{rp|147}} Pura is a small island ({{convert|28|km2|mi2}}) but it is home to both the Reta and Blagar languages, which are related but not mutually intelligible. Inaccessibility and hostility between the villages on Pura allowed the two languages to coexist in close proximity. In modern times, cross-village communication has increased, but has been generally done in the local [[lingua franca]]s: [[Bahasa Indonesia]] and [[Malay language|Malay]].<ref name="Nash2020"/>{{rp|9,15}}

Three villages on Ternate speak Retta, as they were established by Retta-speakers from Pura in the early 20th century, possibly due to religious conflict. Ternate is smaller than Pura, but like Pura, it is multilingual. On the north shore of Ternate, villagers speak [[Alorese language|Alorese]], an unrelated [[Austronesian language]].<ref name=Willemsen2021/>{{rp|147}}<ref name=Sulistyono2021/>{{rp|348-349}}

